        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: restrict non-owner gateway tool inventory [AI]",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users with non-owner Gateway access could have owner-only Gateway control tools included in chat-driven agent runs.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe shared agent tool-policy pipeline now treats an explicit non-owner sender identity as a hard deny for the owner-only Gateway core tools. This uses the existing owner-only tool classification and applies it before the final model tool inventory is returned, including inherited tool surfaces for spawned sessions and scheduled follow-up jobs.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNon-owner Gateway chat turns no longer receive privileged Gateway, scheduler, or node-control tools. Owner/admin Gateway runs and local runs without an explicit non-owner identity keep their existing tool surface.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/agent-tools-agent-config.test.ts` passed: 24 tests.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/agent-tools.create-openclaw-coding-tools.test.ts` passed: 68 tests.\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n\n- Current-head local adapter proof passed for `32c621a38f8968600ba27857eeb2bc792cb18b30`: `node --import tsx --input-type=module --eval '<createOpenClawCodingTools webchat owner/non-owner inventory proof>'` showed owner/admin WebChat kept `cron`, `gateway`, and `nodes`, while explicit non-owner WebChat returned `nonOwnerProtectedTools: []`; the policy log showed `gateway sender owner-only tools.deny` matched those three tools.\n- Remote Testbox proof was attempted but blocked because the local Crabbox binary is 0.20.0 and the repo wrapper requires >=0.22.0 for Blacksmith Testbox; direct AWS Crabbox was also blocked by missing broker login.\n\nAI-assisted: yes.\n\n",
